WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.220
MINHAZ KAZI: Welcome
to DataVis DevTalk.

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:03.970
I'm your host, Minhaz Kazi.

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:06.000
Our today's guest is Sarah Liou.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.310
Sarah lives in Toronto,
Canada, and attends

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:09.780
McMaster University.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:12.240
There she is working towards
a Bachelor of Commerce,

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.100
majoring in marketing.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:18.332
Sarah is also one of the winners
of the Visualize 2030 contest.

00:00:18.332 --> 00:00:20.040
With Sarah's love for
animals and passion

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.200
for the environment, she
was excited to create

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.450
a visual story that
would drive awareness

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:25.770
to these important causes.

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:29.207
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:32.580
Sarah, welcome to the show.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:34.080
SARAH LIOU: Thank
you for having me.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:35.997
MINHAZ KAZI: So tell the
audience a little bit

00:00:35.997 --> 00:00:36.840
about yourself.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.600
What are you busy
with these days?

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.750
SARAH LIOU: Yeah, so I'm
currently a commerce student

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:43.070
at McMaster University.

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:45.980
And I am currently
on internship.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:50.780
So I will be returning to school
for my last year in the fall.

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:52.370
Once I graduate,
I'll hopefully be

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:54.740
looking to start my career
in something in marketing

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:56.762
or maybe something
in the digital space.

00:00:56.762 --> 00:00:58.970
MINHAZ KAZI: So how did you
become interested in data

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:01.050
visualization in
the first place?

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.410
SARAH LIOU: So at my
organization currently

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:07.070
where I'm interning, I have
had a couple of opportunities

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:11.570
to create different
dashboards using Data Studio--

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:14.030
for instance, a sales
performance dashboard

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:17.420
and a dashboard on seeing where
our leads are coming from.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:20.540
So that is what forced
me to learn more

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:21.960
about data visualization.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.860
But also, we-- collectively
as an organization,

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:28.040
we occasionally do
data visualization

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:29.540
for fun and to
practice our skills.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:33.620
So being able to see other
people's more creative works

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:35.330
of data visualization
is what really

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:37.430
got me excited to
try it myself, as I

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:40.220
like creating things that
are visually appealing

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:41.630
and fun to look at.

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:44.470
So that was what really
excited me to learn more.

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:46.970
MINHAZ KAZI: What has been your
experience with Data Studio?

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:49.730
You mentioned that the
organization you're working at,

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:51.570
that's where you got
introduced to the tool.

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.340
But as you've used
it more and more,

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:55.630
what do you think about it?

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:58.600
What has been your overall
experience with this?

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:01.680
SARAH LIOU: Yeah, so Data
Studio is a great tool.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.310
It's basically the only
data visualization tool

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:07.830
that I use because
it's so simple to use.

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:10.080
And it's really
easy to get started.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.260
And you really just
get this blank canvas,

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.130
and you can go
wherever you want.

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:16.940
You can make something
very simple and plain

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:18.403
and just get right to the point.

00:02:18.403 --> 00:02:19.820
Or you can do
something completely

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:21.650
different and
creative and turn it

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:26.580
into kind of like a piece of
art, but also showing data.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:27.950
So that's what I really love.

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:30.560
And, like I said, with
the drag-and-drop features

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:32.570
and the different
filtering options,

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:35.990
you can really completely
transform a basic chart

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:39.890
into something that's
interactive and something that

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:42.410
just kind of goes with the
flow of what you're trying

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:44.120
to tell in your narrative.

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:46.370
MINHAZ KAZI: Congrats on
being one of the five winners

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:47.850
of the Visualize 2030 contest.

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:49.197
SARAH LIOU: Thank you.

00:02:49.197 --> 00:02:50.780
MINHAZ KAZI: Can you
take the audience

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:52.992
through the visualization
that you built?

00:02:52.992 --> 00:02:55.190
Like, tell them about
the story that you're

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:58.573
trying to tell throughout
this entire dashboard.

00:02:58.573 --> 00:03:00.740
SARAH LIOU: Yeah, so let's
go ahead and get into it,

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:02.580
and I can go through that.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:05.490
So here's my data
visualization that I submitted.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:08.360
So I focused on a total of four
of the Sustainable Development

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:09.530
Goals.

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:13.340
So I discuss how by
achieving the one goal--

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.260
one of the targets in
the Zero Hunger goal

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:17.750
is to improve our
agricultural practices

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:19.380
and make them more sustainable.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:21.170
So by achieving this
one, we can work

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:24.500
towards three other goals of
climate action, life on land,

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:26.210
and having clean
water and sanitation

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:27.810
available to everyone.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.290
So as I was going
through, I realized

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:35.150
that agriculture doesn't just
affect the actual climate

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:37.250
change, but it affects
so many different things

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:38.520
in our environment.

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:42.170
So I wanted to display this as a
little roadmap that we can work

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:44.665
towards achieving these
goals in a future state,

00:03:44.665 --> 00:03:46.040
and the different
roadblocks that

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:49.080
are getting in the way of
actually achieving them.

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:53.090
So for instance,
agricultural practices,

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:56.010
they contribute to many
greenhouse gas emissions.

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:58.920
And we can see that
they increase over time.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:03.110
And in regards to livestock
production in particular,

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:05.540
as it increases around the
globe in every single--

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:07.640
in almost every
country, we can see

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:10.250
that there is a positive
correlation between livestock

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:12.860
production, as well as
the total greenhouse gas

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:15.070
emissions in our atmosphere.

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:17.800
And when we focus
on life on land,

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:22.070
we can see that here, in
1990, the agricultural land

00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:25.550
and forest area were
almost equal in regards

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:26.610
to arable land.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:28.820
But if we move to
2015, you can see

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:31.820
that there is a significant
difference between agricultural

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:33.860
and forest area land use.

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:36.200
And I focus in on some
different case studies

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:40.160
to see how there are different
habitat losses because

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:43.070
of the land that is being
used for agricultural purposes

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:44.780
rather than forest.

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:46.790
And in regards to
water resources,

00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:49.460
I focused on some different
case studies as well

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:53.090
and how a majority of water
uses for certain countries

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:55.070
are used for
agriculture, even if they

00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:57.140
have water stress already.

00:04:57.140 --> 00:04:59.990
So at the end here, I just
offered some different calls

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:03.530
to action on what we can
do to achieve these goals.

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:07.340
And by limiting our
meat consumption

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:09.680
and using less land
for agriculture

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:11.180
and less water for
agriculture, we

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:13.850
could really work towards
achieving all four

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:15.720
of these goals in the future.

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:17.720
MINHAZ KAZI: What do you
think about Data Studio

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:22.460
as a tool for anyone who's
trying to tell their own story

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:25.470
and trying to bring
about social change?

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:28.690
SARAH LIOU: Yeah, so like I
said, because Data Studio is

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:32.040
very easy to get started with,
and it's also free to use,

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:32.930
anyone--

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:34.455
especially if you're
in a nonprofit

00:05:34.455 --> 00:05:35.830
or if you're doing
a charity, you

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:40.990
may not have the funds to invest
in a complex data visualization

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:41.740
tool--

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:44.110
Data Studio allows
anyone to learn more

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:47.230
about data visualization and
to actually put their data

00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:48.130
into visualization.

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:52.340
So I think it's a great tool,
especially for those purposes.

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:55.390
And because-- just data
visualization in general,

00:05:55.390 --> 00:05:58.480
when you're putting
the data into visuals,

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:01.090
it's very easy for
anyone to understand.

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:03.730
So you could literally
just have a chart.

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:06.100
And pretty much anyone
can look at a bar chart

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:09.220
and see that one is way
bigger than another.

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:12.220
And especially when it comes to
things like language barriers

00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:15.670
and just different knowledge
of understanding when it comes

00:06:15.670 --> 00:06:18.160
to data, this is a great way
to get your message out there

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:21.160
to everyone, regardless of
how familiar they actually

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:23.350
are with data visualization.

00:06:23.350 --> 00:06:26.800
And it's also a great way to
get your story out there really

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:30.520
quickly, because you can go
from explaining in a paragraph

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:34.330
form of why this is that way,
and that could take, like,

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:35.080
several minutes.

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:37.540
Or you could simply just
glance over at a chart

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:39.670
and pull insights instantly.

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:42.070
And Data Studio makes
that really easy

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:44.173
to construct and get out there.

00:06:44.173 --> 00:06:46.090
And then again, with the
interactive features,

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:48.040
that just makes it so much--

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:49.570
it completely changes
the experience

00:06:49.570 --> 00:06:52.153
of the person who's
viewing the visualization.

00:06:52.153 --> 00:06:54.320
MINHAZ KAZI: What are your
advice for folks who want

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:56.090
to tell their own data stories?

00:06:56.090 --> 00:06:59.750
What kind of tips
would you give them?

00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:01.840
SARAH LIOU: So I
would say definitely

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:03.910
look at some other
examples online

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:06.730
to get some inspiration on how
you actually want to construct

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:09.250
your data story, and
what's the best way to show

00:07:09.250 --> 00:07:10.960
what you're trying to say.

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:13.900
But I would also say, rather
than just copying something

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:16.485
that you see online,
definitely go into the tool,

00:07:16.485 --> 00:07:18.610
whether you're using Data
Studio or something else,

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:20.770
just go in there, look
at the blank canvas,

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:23.050
and play around with
it, and see what

00:07:23.050 --> 00:07:24.520
it is you're trying to show.

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:27.430
And really focus on what
you're trying to tell.

00:07:27.430 --> 00:07:30.430
And you can really frame
the order of your story

00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:32.540
exactly in the story that
you're trying to tell.

00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:34.850
So again, when you're going
through and constructing,

00:07:34.850 --> 00:07:37.780
just think that you're telling a
story to someone that you know,

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:40.523
and construct the
visualization in that order.

00:07:40.523 --> 00:07:42.940
MINHAZ KAZI: Sarah, thank you
again for being on the show.

00:07:42.940 --> 00:07:45.390
You can learn more about
the Visualize 2030 contest

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:47.770
and look at the winning
visualizations in the links

00:07:47.770 --> 00:07:48.700
in the description.

00:07:48.700 --> 00:07:50.930
For more Data Studio
content, be sure to subscribe

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:52.120
to the GCP channel.

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:54.790
And you can always visit
our site at developers.googl

00:07:54.790 --> 00:07:57.070
e.com/datastudio.

00:07:57.070 --> 00:07:59.610
Until next time, keep
building more Vis.

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:02.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

